export const componentAuthorGuide = "# Built-in Components\n\nEach section states one Component's identity, purpose collections, when to use it, when another route serves better, its terminal stance, its accessibility contract, and its canonical examples. Choose by the reader's task, then author through the public contract:\n\n- Runtime: select the slug from a section heading with `emitDesignSystemRuntime` from `@discern-sh/design-system/runtime`. Generated dependency resolution adds prerequisites, and the emitted `discern.css` applies only inside `data-discern-root`.\n- React: import the named adapter from `@discern-sh/design-system/react` and render it at build time.\n- Terminal: import the named renderer from `@discern-sh/design-system/cli`; it is pure and takes explicit terminal capabilities.\n- Semantic HTML: use the `discern-<slug>` class family the package owns. Consumer styles never target owned classes, copy Component CSS, or fork a Component for appearance.\n\n## Purposes\n\n- **Building documentation** (`building-documentation`): Long-form guidance, reference, and documentation chrome. Components: Command (`command`), Command group (`command-group`), Expected result (`expected-result`), Path reference (`path-reference`), Procedure (`procedure`), Procedure step (`procedure-step`), Prerequisite list (`prerequisite-list`), Branch choice (`branch-choice`), Retry notice (`retry-notice`), Destructive action notice (`destructive-action-notice`), Result summary (`result-summary`), Diagnostic (`diagnostic`), Raw output (`raw-output`), Standard meter (`standard-meter`), Artifact tree (`artifact-tree`), File change (`file-change`), Artifact card (`artifact-card`), Ownership badge (`ownership-badge`), Decision record (`decision-record`), Rule (`rule`), Task metadata (`task-metadata`), Agent handoff (`agent-handoff`), Docs header (`docs-header`), Docs nav (`docs-nav`), Search palette (`search-palette`), Anchor heading (`anchor-heading`), Copy button (`copy-button`), Glossary term (`glossary-term`), Article layout (`article-layout`), Prose (`prose`), Markdown (`markdown`), Paragraph (`paragraph`), List (`list`), Blockquote (`blockquote`), Code block (`code-block`), Code listing (`code-listing`), Data figure (`data-figure`), Chart (`chart`), Diagram (`diagram`), Footnotes (`footnotes`).\n- **Displaying tool output** (`displaying-tool-output`): Runs, diagnostics, artifacts, and machine evidence. Components: Overflow cue (`overflow-cue`), Window (`window`), Terminal (`terminal`), Table (`table`), Stat (`stat`), Sparkline (`sparkline`), Diffstat (`diffstat`), Banner (`banner`), Worklog (`worklog`), Transcript (`transcript`), Verification report (`verification-report`), Fleet (`fleet`), Command (`command`), Expected result (`expected-result`), Path reference (`path-reference`), Result summary (`result-summary`), Diagnostic (`diagnostic`), Raw output (`raw-output`), Standard meter (`standard-meter`), Activity log (`activity-log`), Artifact tree (`artifact-tree`), File change (`file-change`), Artifact card (`artifact-card`), Ownership badge (`ownership-badge`), Decision record (`decision-record`), Rule (`rule`), Markdown (`markdown`), Chart (`chart`), Diagram (`diagram`).\n- **Procedural workflow** (`procedural-workflow`): Executable steps, choices, recovery, and proof. Components: Command (`command`), Command group (`command-group`), Expected result (`expected-result`), Path reference (`path-reference`), Procedure (`procedure`), Procedure step (`procedure-step`), Prerequisite list (`prerequisite-list`), Branch choice (`branch-choice`), Retry notice (`retry-notice`), Destructive action notice (`destructive-action-notice`), Result summary (`result-summary`), Diagnostic (`diagnostic`), Activity log (`activity-log`), Task metadata (`task-metadata`), Agent handoff (`agent-handoff`).\n- **Marketing site** (`marketing-site`): Product narrative, trust, comparison, and conversion. Components: Brand (`brand`), Grid (`grid`), Masonry (`masonry`), Section (`section`), Badge (`badge`), Card (`card`), Window (`window`), Terminal (`terminal`), Stat (`stat`), Sparkline (`sparkline`), Backdrop (`backdrop`), Survey backdrop (`survey-backdrop`), Approach backdrop (`approach-backdrop`), Fold backdrop (`fold-backdrop`), Aperture backdrop (`aperture-backdrop`), Impression backdrop (`impression-backdrop`), Envelope backdrop (`envelope-backdrop`), Cleave backdrop (`cleave-backdrop`), Tiling backdrop (`tiling-backdrop`), Compression backdrop (`compression-backdrop`), Harmonic backdrop (`harmonic-backdrop`), Marketing section (`marketing-section`), Site header (`site-header`), Marketing intro (`marketing-intro`), Hero block (`hero-block`), Logo cloud (`logo-cloud`), Audience grid (`audience-grid`), Feature bento (`feature-bento`), Process steps (`process-steps`), Comparison table (`comparison-table`), CTA band (`cta-band`), Site footer (`site-footer`), Marketing stage (`marketing-stage`), Editorial hero (`editorial-hero`), Narrative chapter (`narrative-chapter`), Journey overview (`journey-overview`), Outcome spotlight (`outcome-spotlight`), Voice break (`voice-break`), Closing statement (`closing-statement`), Code listing (`code-listing`).\n\n## Core\n\n### Icon (`icon`)\n\nVendor-neutral sizing and accessibility wrapper for an injected icon graphic.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Icon`. Terminal: `renderIconCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Decorative icons are hidden automatically.\n- Meaningful icons require a label.\n\nExamples: Labelled icon (`default`).\n\n### Button (`button`)\n\nTyped button and anchor variants with vendor-neutral leading and trailing icon slots.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Button`. Terminal: `renderButtonCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Anchor and button props are mutually exclusive.\n- Visible focus and disabled states are built in.\n\nExamples: Primary (`default`), Secondary (`secondary`), Ghost (`ghost`), Danger (`danger`).\n\n### Icon button (`icon-button`)\n\nSquare icon action with a required accessible label and injected graphic.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: none.\nReact: `IconButton`. Terminal: `renderIconButtonCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- A text label is required even when only an icon is visible.\n\nExamples: Quiet (`default`), Outline (`outline`).\n\n### Theme toggle (`theme-toggle`)\n\nControlled two-state light/dark comfort adjustment with outlined and quiet treatments; the consumer owns system resolution and persistence.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: none.\nReact: `ThemeToggle`. Terminal: `renderThemeToggleCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A persistent page-chrome control should make an immediate light/dark comfort adjustment.\n- The interface only needs to expose the opposite of the currently resolved light/dark theme.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Theme switcher in a settings or inspection surface where System, Light, and Dark are deliberate choices.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The accessible name states the destination theme and swaps with the state, so the action is always explicit.\n- The destination-style name makes this an action button, not an aria-pressed toggle button with a stable name.\n- The glyph is decorative and hidden; the component never mutates the document itself, keeping theme application observable by the consumer.\n\nExamples: From light (`default`), Quiet (`quiet`), From dark (`from-dark`).\n\n### Theme switcher (`theme-switcher`)\n\nControlled System, Light, and Dark preference group for settings and deterministic theme inspection.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: none.\nReact: `ThemeSwitcher`. Terminal: exempt — Terminal colour selection is already a caller-owned renderer input; presenting a System, Light, and Dark form would falsely imply an input driver and persistence policy.\n\nUse when:\n- A settings surface lets users make an explicit future-facing appearance preference.\n- A design or inspection tool needs deterministic System, Light, and Dark choices.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Theme toggle for a persistent header or footer control whose job is an immediate comfort adjustment.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Native radio inputs preserve one-of-three selection semantics and keyboard navigation.\n- A fieldset and visually hidden legend name the preference group; every choice retains a visible text label.\n- The consumer owns persistence and applies system, light, or dark to its opted-in root.\n\nExamples: Theme preference (`default`, web only).\n\n### Logo (`logo`)\n\nAdaptive logo wrapper for text glyphs, injected graphics, and wide or square marks.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Logo`. Terminal: `renderLogoCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Omit label when the logo is decorative beside a visible brand name; provide label only when the logo carries the identity by itself.\n- Injected images should use empty alternative text because the wrapper owns the accessible label.\n\nExamples: Plain (`default`), Tile (`tile`), Square (`square`).\n\n### Brand (`brand`)\n\nComposable brand lockup pairing an adaptive decorative mark with a name and optional tagline.\n\nGroup: Core. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Brand`. Terminal: `renderBrandCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The visible name carries the identity, so the composed Logo mark is always decorative and never announced twice.\n- Wrap Brand in the destination link instead of nesting a link inside the lockup.\n\nExamples: Wordmark (`default`), With tagline (`tagline`), Name only (`name-only`).\n\n## Layout\n\n### Container (`container`)\n\nCentred responsive content boundary with named readable widths.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Container`. Terminal: `renderContainerCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Measure (`default`), Full width (`full`).\n\n### Stack (`stack`)\n\nVertical composition using token-constrained gaps and explicit alignment.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Stack`. Terminal: `renderStackCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Stack (`default`), Centred (`centred`).\n\n### Cluster (`cluster`)\n\nWrapping horizontal composition for actions, tags, and compact groups.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Cluster`. Terminal: `renderClusterCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Actions (`default`), Centred (`centred`).\n\n### Grid (`grid`)\n\nIntrinsic responsive grid without breakpoint-specific column props.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Grid`. Terminal: `renderGridCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Responsive grid (`default`), Single column (`single-column`).\n\n### Masonry (`masonry`)\n\nStandards-first variable-height columns with native Grid Lanes and a packed CSS fallback.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Masonry`. Terminal: `renderMasonryCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Independent peer cards or media have useful natural heights and should pack without row alignment.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Items form an ordered procedure, ranked result set, comparison, or focus-heavy navigation sequence.\n- A curated hierarchy must fill a strict rectangle; use Feature bento instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Neutral item wrappers retain child semantics and DOM order in every layout mode.\n- The CSS Columns fallback flows down columns, while native Grid Lanes places each next item in the shortest lane; use only for independent peers whose meaning does not depend on visual adjacency.\n\nExamples: Variable height (`default`), Single column (`single-column`).\n\n### Section (`section`)\n\nSemantic page section with tokenized surface and vertical rhythm.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Section`. Terminal: `renderSectionCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Labelled section (`default`), Sunken (`sunken`).\n\n### Overflow cue (`overflow-cue`)\n\nNative scrolling with decorative cues for the logical edges that still contain content.\n\nGroup: Layout. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `OverflowCue`. Terminal: exempt — Overflow cue is a browser scroll affordance driven by element geometry; terminal renderers expose clipping, folds, and overflow through their own frame facts.\nBrowser behavior: `overflow-cue` (load the emitted `discern.js` beside `discern.css`).\n\nUse when:\n- A bounded region can scroll and its continuation is otherwise easy to miss.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Content fits without scrolling or the interface needs pagination, virtualisation, or carousel controls.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The cue is decorative and pointer-transparent; the native scroll container, scrollbar, wheel, touch, trackpad, keyboard, and programmatic scrolling remain available.\n- Give an owned scroll container a concise viewportLabel. Raw HTML and descendant-target consumers must make and name their existing target keyboard-focusable.\n- Without JavaScript every edge state remains false, so content still scrolls without a misleading cue.\n\nExamples: Vertical · start (`vertical-start`, web only), Vertical · middle (`vertical-middle`, web only), Vertical · end (`vertical-end`, web only), Horizontal · start (`horizontal-start`, web only), Horizontal · middle (`horizontal-middle`, web only), Horizontal · end (`horizontal-end`, web only), Both axes (`both-axes`, web only), Dynamic content and size (`dynamic-content`, web only), RTL inline overflow (`rtl-inline`, web only), No overflow (`no-overflow`, web only).\n\n## Display\n\n### Badge (`badge`)\n\nCompact status and metadata label with semantic tones.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Badge`. Terminal: `renderBadgeCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A compact visible label communicates status, category, or arbitrary metadata.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The label must distinguish authored, generated, project-owned, or tool-owned provenance; use Ownership badge.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Accent (`default`), Neutral (`neutral`), Success (`success`), Warning (`warning`), Danger (`danger`).\n\n### Card (`card`)\n\nComposable surface with explicit elevation, texture, and padding choices.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Card`. Terminal: `renderCardCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Plain (`default`), Raised (`raised`), Dotted texture (`dotted`).\n\n### Divider (`divider`)\n\nQuiet editorial rule with an optional annotation label.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Divider`. Terminal: `renderDividerCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Rule (`default`), Labelled (`labelled`).\n\n### Heading (`heading`)\n\nNative level-aware heading with React phrasing content and rich, lossless terminal wrapping.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Heading`. Terminal: `renderHeadingCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A titled section needs a real heading level in the document outline.\n- Rich inline meaning or a complete long title must survive terminal wrapping.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Kicker or ordinary lead text when the copy labels content without establishing a section.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Choose levels 1–6 for semantic outline order; accent treatment never replaces the heading level.\n- Terminal wrapping preserves the complete label and aligns continuations beneath its content prefix.\n\nExamples: Section heading (`default`), Accented phrase (`accent`), Rich inline content (`rich-content`).\n\n### Kicker (`kicker`)\n\nUppercase annotation label with an optional index.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Kicker`. Terminal: `renderKickerCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Plain (`default`), Indexed (`indexed`).\n\n### Tag (`tag`)\n\nCompact metadata chip with an optional accessible remove action.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Tag`. Terminal: `renderTagCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Plain (`default`), Removable (`removable`).\n\n### Window (`window`)\n\nFramed presentation surface with standard and campaign showcase treatments for product UI and code examples.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: displaying-tool-output, marketing-site.\nReact: `Window`. Terminal: `renderWindowCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A visual needs recognizable browser-style chrome; use showcase for wide campaign evidence with optional trailing status or actions.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Standard (`standard`), Showcase (`showcase`).\n\n### Terminal (`terminal`)\n\nFramed monospace surface for commands and terminal output, including a stable dark campaign showcase.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: displaying-tool-output, marketing-site.\nReact: `Terminal`. Terminal: `renderTerminalCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- You need to reproduce a terminal session or present interleaved commands and output as one framed surface.\n- A marketing page needs structured tool output with optional trailing chrome and a compact explanatory footer; use showcase.\n\nDo not use when:\n- A reader must copy and run one exact command with working-directory, expected-result, or failure context; use Command.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Output preserves whitespace and scrolls horizontally instead of wrapping long lines.\n- Provide a concise title when the terminal output needs additional context.\n- Showcase keeps output light-on-dark in both themes while preserving the same preformatted text.\n\nExamples: Standard (`standard`), Showcase (`showcase`).\n\n### Table (`table`)\n\nGeneral-purpose semantic data table with native browser markup and compact or lossless responsive terminal layouts.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Table`. Terminal: `renderTableCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Headers define relationships across ordinary relational data, including rich phrasing, empty values, or content that must remain complete at narrow terminal widths.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Comparison table for a curated two-option marketing comparison with a title and feature narrative.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Consumers author real thead/tbody/th markup, so header associations stay native; the wrapper only owns overflow and styling.\n- Horizontal overflow scrolls inside the wrapper rather than the page, and the optional caption keeps its semantic position.\n- Cells retain ordinary word boundaries even when an enclosing prose layout permits emergency wrapping; wide values use the table's local horizontal viewport.\n- The responsive terminal layout retains every header/value relationship in labelled records when a coherent grid cannot fit, without relying on colour.\n\nExamples: Three-column data (`default`), Rich and empty cells (`rich-cells`), Dense narrow width (`dense-overflow`).\n\n### Stat (`stat`)\n\nOne labelled figure with an optional trend-coloured context line.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: displaying-tool-output, marketing-site.\nReact: `Stat`. Terminal: `renderStatCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Label, value, and context read as one continuous text run in source order.\n- Trend colour is reinforced by the context wording itself, never carried by colour alone.\n\nExamples: Neutral (`default`), Positive trend (`positive`), Negative trend (`negative`), With sparkline (`with-sparkline`).\n\n### Sparkline (`sparkline`)\n\nCompact recent-movement graphic with a mandatory endpoint annotation.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: displaying-tool-output, marketing-site.\nReact: `Sparkline`. Terminal: `renderSparklineCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Showing recent movement beside a headline number, such as a Stat trend.\n- Giving a dense list one glanceable direction cue per row.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Reading exact values or intermediate points — a sparkline is lossy by design, and only its endpoint annotation states numbers; use the line chart kind.\n- Comparing two series — each sparkline scales to its own extremes, so two sparklines are never comparable.\n- Inline terminal prose — the terminal form is a block-context run, never an inline grammar.\n- Showing more than 100 observations — the fixed browser view cannot distinguish a denser run; summarize the period or use the line chart kind.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The endpoint annotation is visible text carrying the numeric truth, so no reading depends on glyph fidelity or colour.\n- The movement graphic is decorative and hidden from assistive technology; the annotation reads in source order.\n\nExamples: Movement (`default`), With gaps (`with-gaps`), Flat (`flat`), Decline (`decline`).\n\n### Diffstat (`diffstat`)\n\nInline added/removed change summary — signed counts beside proportional squares, with both sides kept visible when either is non-zero.\n\nGroup: Display. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Diffstat`. Terminal: `renderDiffstatCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The signed counts are real text and the whole meaning; the squares are hidden decoration.\n- Direction is carried by the plus and minus signs, never by colour alone.\n\nExamples: Mixed changes (`default`), Additions only (`added`), Removals only (`removed`), No changes (`empty`).\n\n## Artwork\n\n### Backdrop (`backdrop`)\n\nDecorative, theme-aware background plane with a shared presence and motion contract for authored artwork.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `Backdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Backdrop owns viewport-scaled browser positioning and decorative layer presence; terminal output has no background plane or semantic content to render in its place.\n\nUse when:\n- A section needs token-driven decorative artwork behind complete foreground content.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The visual communicates evidence or meaning; use a semantic figure with alternative text instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Backdrops are always hidden from assistive technology and never receive focus.\n- Authored motion resolves to a complete still composition when reduced motion is requested.\n- Decorative backdrops disappear in forced-colour modes so they cannot obscure foreground content.\n\nExamples: Backdrop layer (`default`, web only).\n\n### Survey backdrop (`survey-backdrop`)\n\nTileable equilateral ruling crossed by one slow travelling band of light.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `SurveyBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Survey backdrop's tileable equilateral ruling and travelling light wash depend on a continuous decorative browser plane unavailable to terminal output.\n\nUse when:\n- A broad field needs quiet measurement, registration, and a slow directional wash.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The composition needs a clear focal object rather than an even field.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Survey field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Approach backdrop (`approach-backdrop`)\n\nNested right-anchored triangles whose accent moves between non-adjacent depths.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `ApproachBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Approach backdrop's nested right-anchored triangles and accent moving between visual depths require scalable browser geometry with no honest terminal-cell equivalent.\n\nUse when:\n- Foreground copy needs a clear column while a constructed focal point gathers at the edge.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The decorative field must remain even across the complete canvas.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Approach field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Fold backdrop (`fold-backdrop`)\n\nTriangular tessellation whose authored facets take light one diagonal band at a time.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `FoldBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Fold backdrop's triangular tessellation conveys material depth through continuously lit facets, decorative browser geometry that terminal glyphs cannot preserve.\n\nUse when:\n- A full-bleed surface needs low-contrast material depth behind short foreground copy.\n\nDo not use when:\n- CSS payload must be minimal; this is the densest backdrop in the family.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Fold field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Aperture backdrop (`aperture-backdrop`)\n\nOff-centre triangular opening that admits three unequal raking beams.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `ApertureBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Aperture backdrop's off-centre triangular opening and raking light beams require a scalable layered plane that terminal cells cannot represent without inventing semantic content.\n\nUse when:\n- A section needs an atmospheric incident concentrated across its upper band.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The visual must cover the full height with an even texture.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Aperture field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Impression backdrop (`impression-backdrop`)\n\nConfigurable glyph lattice whose soft accent aperture follows the reader without script.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `ImpressionBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Impression backdrop's pointer-responsive accent aperture moves across a scalable glyph lattice, while terminal rendering has no equivalent decorative pointer plane.\n\nUse when:\n- A typographic field should respond softly to pointer position while remaining complete when still.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The repeated mark carries semantic meaning or needs to be announced.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Impression field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Envelope backdrop (`envelope-backdrop`)\n\nStraight chord families that imply curves while one light crest travels their tangents.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `EnvelopeBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Envelope backdrop's chord families imply curves through precise scalable tangencies and a travelling light crest, relationships a terminal-cell rendering would misstate.\n\nUse when:\n- A corner or broad canvas needs mathematical density with no filled surface.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The foreground needs a uniformly quiet field with no directional incident.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Envelope field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Cleave backdrop (`cleave-backdrop`)\n\nOversized split geometry that leaves two low-contrast surfaces meeting at one median.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `CleaveBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Cleave backdrop's oversized low-contrast surfaces must fill a browser plane and meet at a responsive median, a decorative spatial relationship terminal cells cannot preserve.\n\nUse when:\n- A restrained full-bleed field needs area and tension rather than repeated linework.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The section needs an intricate texture or strongly animated focal point.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Cleave field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Tiling backdrop (`tiling-backdrop`)\n\nTruchet arc field whose highlighted route reconnects as isolated tiles turn.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `TilingBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Tiling backdrop's rotating Truchet arcs preserve a highlighted route through scalable tiles; terminal glyphs would turn that decorative motion into misleading route data.\n\nUse when:\n- A broad field needs quiet repetition with a sparse route that changes without breaking.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The visual must explain a real network, path, or routing decision.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Tiling field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Compression backdrop (`compression-backdrop`)\n\nTicked ribbon whose concertina fold compresses while its flat runs preserve the apparent length.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `CompressionBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Compression backdrop's ticked ribbon depends on a scalable concertina fold and continuous flat runs; a terminal frame cannot express that decorative geometry honestly.\n\nUse when:\n- A wide surface needs one measured linear gesture with slow, material movement.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The background needs an even texture across the complete canvas.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Compression field (`default`, web only).\n\n### Harmonic backdrop (`harmonic-backdrop`)\n\nFine grains disperse and settle into two nodal modes across the full backdrop.\n\nGroup: Artwork. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `HarmonicBackdrop`. Terminal: exempt — Harmonic backdrop's fine grains disperse and settle into spatial nodal modes across a browser plane; terminal text would falsely imply measured data or content.\n\nUse when:\n- A broad canvas needs a fine atmospheric field whose motion periodically resolves into structure.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The grain positions would be read as measured evidence or a physical simulation.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The composition is always hidden from assistive technology and never receives focus.\n- Reduced-motion preferences preserve the complete authored still rather than an empty state.\n- Forced-colour modes omit the decorative layer so foreground content remains unambiguous.\n\nExamples: Harmonic field (`default`, web only).\n\n## Forms\n\n### Field (`field`)\n\nShared label, hint, required, and error structure for custom controls.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Field`. Terminal: `renderFieldCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Labels and messages use deterministic control IDs.\n\nExamples: Idle (`default`), Active (`active`), Filled (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`), Acknowledgement (`acknowledgement`).\n\n### Input (`input`)\n\nNative input with typed label, help, required, and invalid relationships.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Input`. Terminal: `renderInputCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Idle (`default`), Active (`active`), Filled (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`), Searching (`searching`).\n\n### Textarea (`textarea`)\n\nResizable multiline input sharing the Field accessibility contract.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Textarea`. Terminal: `renderTextareaCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Idle (`default`), Active (`active`), Filled (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`), Tall window (`tall-window`).\n\n### Select (`select`)\n\nStyled native select with typed options and associated field messaging.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Select`. Terminal: `renderSelectCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Idle (`default`), Grouped options (`grouped`), Active (`active`), Selected (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`).\n\n### Checkbox (`checkbox`)\n\nNative checkbox with an associated visible label and optional description.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Checkbox`. Terminal: `renderCheckboxCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Unchecked (`default`), Grouped choices (`grouped`), Active (`active`), Checked (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`).\n\n### Radio (`radio`)\n\nNative radio option sharing the labelled Choice structure.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Radio`. Terminal: `renderRadioCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Idle (`default`), Grouped choices (`grouped`), Active (`active`), Selected (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`).\n\n### Switch (`switch`)\n\nNative checkbox exposed as a switch, with associated label and description.\n\nGroup: Forms. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Switch`. Terminal: `renderSwitchCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Off (`default`), Active (`active`), On (`filled`), Validation error (`validation-error`), Disabled (`disabled`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`).\n\n## Feedback\n\n### Banner (`banner`)\n\nInline semantic message with neutral, accent, success, warning, and danger tones.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Banner`. Terminal: `renderBannerCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A page or region needs a concise announcement whose meaning is complete without reproduction details.\n\nDo not use when:\n- A specific failure needs location, evidence, reproduction, correction, and retry guidance; use Diagnostic.\n\nAccessibility: Metadata records no Component-specific note; the package invariants still apply.\n\nExamples: Neutral (`default`), Accent (`accent`), Success (`success`), Warning (`warning`), Danger (`danger`).\n\n### Toast (`toast`)\n\nTransient status message plus a labelled live-region container.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Toast`. Terminal: `renderToastCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Danger uses alert semantics; other tones use status semantics.\n- Auto-dismiss is opt-in.\n\nExamples: Neutral (`default`), Success (`success`), Warning (`warning`), Danger (`danger`).\n\n### Tooltip (`tooltip`)\n\nHover and focus tooltip that connects its bubble through aria-describedby.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Tooltip`. Terminal: exempt — A tooltip is hidden supplementary text disclosed by browser hover or focus; terminal output has no equivalent trigger-to-bubble interaction and should print essential guidance directly.\nBrowser behavior: `floating-surface` (load the emitted `discern.js` beside `discern.css`).\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The trigger must remain independently focusable when it represents an action.\n- The emitted browser behavior promotes supported bubbles to the top layer; the static CSS fallback remains available without JavaScript.\n\nExamples: Icon trigger (`default`, web only), Bottom placement (`bottom`, web only).\n\n### Hover card (`hover-card`)\n\nHover- and focus-revealed supplementary surface accepting arbitrary inline or block content.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: none.\nReact: `HoverCard`. Terminal: exempt — Its identity is supplementary content revealed by pointer hover or browser focus; a terminal has neither that hidden spatial relationship nor a faithful static equivalent.\nBrowser behavior: `floating-surface` (load the emitted `discern.js` beside `discern.css`).\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The trigger must be keyboard focusable; focus-within keeps interactive panel content available while users tab through it.\n- aria-details preserves any existing relationship and associates the trigger with the richer panel without misusing the tooltip role.\n- Use inline layout for phrasing content inside prose and block layout for structured content such as headings, paragraphs, lists, and actions.\n- The emitted browser behavior promotes supported panels to the top layer, keeping them clear of clipping ancestors and inside the viewport; the static CSS fallback remains available without JavaScript.\n\nExamples: Rich details (`default`, web only), Inline annotation (`inline`, web only), Overflow boundary (`overflow`, web only).\n\n### Dialog (`dialog`)\n\nControlled native modal dialog with platform focus containment and explicit close behaviour.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Dialog`. Terminal: `renderDialogCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Uses showModal(), aria-labelledby, Escape handling, and a labelled close button.\n- Background scrolling is locked while open.\n\nExamples: Open (`default`), Submitted (`submitted`), Cancelled (`cancelled`).\n\n### Meter (`meter`)\n\nLabelled fraction-of-a-range meter with an optional textual reading beside the bar.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Meter`. Terminal: `renderMeterCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The bar carries role=meter with now/min/max values and an accessible name, so the reading is programmatic, not visual-only.\n- Warning and danger tones pair with the textual reading; the fill colour is never the only signal.\n\nExamples: Empty (`default`), In progress (`quarter`), Complete (`complete`), Warning (`warning`), Danger (`danger`).\n\n### Empty state (`empty-state`)\n\nCentred placeholder for a region with nothing to show yet, with optional icon and follow-up actions.\n\nGroup: Feedback. Purposes: none.\nReact: `EmptyState`. Terminal: `renderEmptyStateCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Title and description are ordinary paragraphs in reading order; the decorative icon is hidden from assistive technology.\n- Follow-up actions are slotted real controls that keep their own semantics and focus behaviour.\n\nExamples: Empty (`default`), Compact (`compact`).\n\n## Navigation\n\n### Tabs (`tabs`)\n\nControlled or uncontrolled tab set with roving focus and complete horizontal keyboard navigation.\n\nGroup: Navigation. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Tabs`. Terminal: `renderTabsCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Arrow keys, Home, End, Enter, and Space follow the ARIA tabs pattern.\n- Tabs and panels have deterministic labelled relationships.\n\nExamples: Overview selected (`default`), Details selected (`details`), Manual activation (`manual`).\n\n### Breadcrumbs (`breadcrumbs`)\n\nScrollable hierarchical page location with linked ancestors and one explicit current page.\n\nGroup: Navigation. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Breadcrumbs`. Terminal: `renderBreadcrumbsCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- A configurable label names the navigation landmark, and the hierarchy remains an ordered list.\n- Only the final, unlinked item carries aria-current=page; visual separators are hidden from assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Compact (`default`), Deep hierarchy (`deep`).\n\n## People\n\n### Avatar (`avatar`)\n\nIdentity mark for one person: portrait photo or UI monogram in five sizes, with an optional presence badge.\n\nGroup: People. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Avatar`. Terminal: `renderAvatarCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- A named avatar carries role=\"img\" with the person's name — and presence, when set — as its accessible label; set decorative when adjacent text already names the person.\n- Presence is never colour alone: the labelled form folds it into the accessible name, and composers such as Persona render their own text alternative.\n- The monogram is aria-hidden decoration derived from the name, so assistive technology hears the name, never the letters.\n\nExamples: Initials with presence (`default`), Portrait (`portrait`, web only), Square (`square`).\n\n### Avatar group (`avatar-group`)\n\nOverlapping stack of Avatars with ring separation and a labelled overflow count for the people it clamps away.\n\nGroup: People. Purposes: none.\nReact: `AvatarGroup`. Terminal: `renderAvatarGroupCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Pass label to name the stack; it then renders role=\"group\" so assistive technology announces the collection once.\n- The overflow chip is itself a labelled Avatar whose accessible name states how many people are hidden.\n- Stacking follows DOM order, so reading order and the visual overlap always agree.\n\nExamples: Overflowing group (`default`), Compact group (`compact`).\n\n### Persona (`persona`)\n\nIdentity lockup pairing an Avatar with a name and secondary detail line — the row form for tables, headers, and comment threads.\n\nGroup: People. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Persona`. Terminal: `renderPersonaCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The visible name is the single source of identity: the built-in avatar renders decorative so nothing is announced twice.\n- Presence is spoken as visually hidden text directly after the name, never conveyed by colour alone.\n- Name and detail truncate visually with an ellipsis while the full text stays available to assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Identity (`default`), With presence (`with-presence`), Long name (`long-name`).\n\n### Mention (`mention`)\n\nInline person chip for running prose — an @sigil or tiny portrait beside the name, em-scaled so it sits in text at any size.\n\nGroup: People. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Mention`. Terminal: `renderMentionCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- With href it renders a real link whose accessible name is exactly the person's name; the sigil and portrait are hidden decoration.\n- Without href it renders a plain span, so a mention never fakes interactivity it does not have.\n- Accent ink on the accent surface holds contrast in both themes, and the hover state deepens both together.\n\nExamples: Inline mention (`default`), Avatar mention (`avatar`).\n\n### Byline (`byline`)\n\nEditorial attribution row: an optional lede, the authors, and middot-separated meta segments such as dates and reading time.\n\nGroup: People. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Byline`. Terminal: `renderBylineCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Renders the HTML address element — the semantic author container for its nearest article — with the italic default reset.\n- Separator middots are CSS pseudo-content with empty alternative text, so assistive technology reads clean segments.\n- Wrap dates in time elements inside the meta slot so machines read them as machines and people read them as prose.\n\nExamples: Article byline (`default`).\n\n### Profile card (`profile-card`)\n\nTeam-member card: square editorial portrait, serif name, kicker-style detail, short bio, and a links slot — portrait or landscape.\n\nGroup: People. Purposes: none.\nReact: `ProfileCard`. Terminal: `renderProfileCardCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The name is an h3 heading at the same depth marketing grid items use; compose cards beneath an h2 section title.\n- The built-in portrait is decorative — the visible printed name carries the identity.\n- The links slot expects real anchors from the consumer; the card adds layout, never click behaviour.\n\nExamples: Portrait layout (`default`), Landscape layout (`landscape`).\n\n## Agents\n\n### Agent avatar (`agent-avatar`)\n\nDark square tile with an interface-type sigil identifying one agent — the machine counterpart to Avatar, so mixed rosters read at a glance.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: none.\nReact: `AgentAvatar`. Terminal: `renderAgentAvatarCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The status light's state joins the accessible label — never conveyed by colour alone — and its pulse runs only when motion is unreduced.\n- Set decorative when a visible printed name carries the identity, so nothing is announced twice.\n- The tile speaks Avatar's sizing and ring contract, so Avatar group stacks people and agents together without extra styling.\n\nExamples: Identity (`default`), Working (`working`), Blocked (`blocked`).\n\n### Agent persona (`agent-persona`)\n\nIdentity lockup pairing an Agent avatar with a name and detail line — the row form for fleet views, tables, and activity chrome.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: none.\nReact: `AgentPersona`. Terminal: `renderAgentPersonaCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The visible name is the single source of identity: the built-in avatar renders decorative so nothing is announced twice.\n- Status is printed directly after the name, so the avatar's colour and glyph only reinforce a visible state word.\n- Name and detail truncate visually with an ellipsis while the full text stays available to assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Agent identity (`default`), Working agent (`working`).\n\n### Agent mention (`agent-mention`)\n\nInline agent chip for running prose — an agent sigil beside the name, em-scaled so it sits in text at any size.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: none.\nReact: `AgentMention`. Terminal: `renderAgentMentionCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- With href it renders a real link whose accessible name is exactly the agent's name; the sigil and tile are hidden decoration.\n- Without href it renders a plain span, so a mention never fakes interactivity it does not have.\n- The chip keeps body ink on the sunken surface in both themes; only the decorative sigil carries accent colour.\n\nExamples: Static mention (`default`), Linked mention (`linked`).\n\n### Worklog (`worklog`)\n\nCompact statused feed of a run's steps — done, active, queued, failed, or skipped — with text annotations and right-aligned timing.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Worklog`. Terminal: `renderWorklogCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Each entry's visible marker glyph carries an accessible status name beside the printed task label.\n- Status pairs a distinct glyph shape and accessible name with its colour, so no state is conveyed by colour alone.\n- Renders an ordered list, so assistive technology announces the run's length and each step's place in it.\n\nExamples: Active run (`default`), Failed run (`failure`).\n\n### Transcript (`transcript`)\n\nOrdered conversation turns between named speakers — compose Persona and Agent persona headers to show humans and agents working together.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Transcript`. Terminal: `renderTranscriptCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Renders an ordered list, so assistive technology announces the conversation's length and each turn's place in it.\n- Each turn's speaker slot carries the identity; compose Persona or Agent persona so the printed name stays the single announced source.\n- Wrap timestamps in the aside slot in time elements so machines read them as machines and people read them as prose.\n\nExamples: Review handoff (`default`).\n\n### Verification report (`verification-report`)\n\nDurable multi-check report with a stamped title, metadata rows, and dot-leadered results recording what was verified and how it ended.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `VerificationReport`. Terminal: `renderVerificationReportCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A durable handoff must record several checks and their outcomes with branch, commit, timing, or change metadata.\n\nDo not use when:\n- You need the plain-language outcome and next action for one tool run; use Result summary.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Check outcomes are spoken as visually hidden text after each value; the glyphs are hidden decoration paired with colour, never colour alone.\n- Metadata and checks render as definition lists, so each label stays programmatically bound to its value.\n- The dot leaders are painted decoration behind the text, invisible to assistive technology and absent in forced-colour modes.\n\nExamples: Passing report (`default`), Failing report (`failure`).\n\n### Fleet (`fleet`)\n\nBoard of parallel efforts — one row per worktree pairing a persona with a monospace branch, a state slot, ahead/behind drift, and timing.\n\nGroup: Agents. Purposes: displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Fleet`. Terminal: `renderFleetCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Ahead/behind arrows are hidden decoration; the counts are spoken as visually hidden words after them.\n- Pass label to name the board; assistive technology then announces the list and its length under that name.\n- The persona and state slots carry their own semantics — compose Agent persona and Badge so identity and status stay announced once each.\n\nExamples: Parallel work (`default`), Long identities (`lossless-identities`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Command (`command`)\n\nExecutable input with working-directory and platform context, an explanation, embedded expected proof, a failure note, and a clean-copy affordance.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `Command`. Terminal: `renderCommandCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A reader must run one exact command with its working directory, platform, expected proof, or failure guidance beside it.\n\nDo not use when:\n- You need to reproduce a whole terminal session or interleave commands with output; use Terminal.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The command is preformatted, never wraps, and its focusable region scrolls horizontally from the keyboard when it exceeds the available width.\n- No shell-prefix glyph is rendered, so both copied and assistive-technology text contain only the executable input.\n- The adapter-only copy action announces its transient text state politely and leaves keyboard focus on the button.\n- Failure guidance uses an explicit text label alongside attention colour.\n\nExamples: Command with context (`default`), Expected proof and failure guidance (`failure`), Long command overflow (`overflow`).\n\n### Command group (`command-group`)\n\nNamed command alternatives stacked with clear labels, keeping every option readable and executable in static HTML without tab behaviour.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `CommandGroup`. Terminal: `renderCommandGroupCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Readers need labelled command alternatives for different platforms or equivalent tools.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The commands must run in sequence; place Command components inside Procedure steps.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Alternatives remain an ordered list rather than becoming hidden tab panels.\n- A string title labels the group automatically; non-text titles should be paired with an explicit aria-label.\n- Each alternative retains the Command component's complete run context and copy behaviour.\n\nExamples: Verification choices (`default`).\n\n### Expected result (`expected-result`)\n\nObservable output or a stated end state that tells a reader how to recognise a successful command or procedure.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `ExpectedResult`. Terminal: `renderExpectedResultCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A command or procedure needs observable proof that tells the reader what success should look like.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The outcome has already been measured; use Result summary for a run or Verification report for a multi-check record.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The visible label names the excerpt as an expectation rather than verified live status.\n- Output remains semantic preformatted code and its focusable region scrolls horizontally from the keyboard instead of wrapping.\n- No success colour appears until a consumer has actually verified the outcome.\n\nExamples: Expected output (`output`), Expected state (`state`).\n\n### Path reference (`path-reference`)\n\nInline file or directory path with middle truncation and an opt-in adapter-only copy affordance, keeping quiet prose inert while owning the correct clipboard payload when copying matters.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `PathReference`. Terminal: `renderPathReferenceCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Prose, a command, or a diagnostic must identify one exact file or directory without losing either end of a long path.\n\nDo not use when:\n- You need to summarise a created or changed artifact with ownership and provenance; use Artifact card.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The full path remains accessible text and a title even when its visible middle is truncated.\n- Truncation preserves the rootward prefix and final file or directory segment at narrow widths.\n- The optional copy action announces its text state and copies the full untruncated path.\n\nExamples: Inline path (`default`), Long copyable path (`long-path`).\n\n### Procedure (`procedure`)\n\nComplete operational sequence with prerequisites, semantic ordered steps, recovery paths, and explicit evidence of completion.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `Procedure`. Terminal: `renderProcedureCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A reader must carry out an ordered operational task with prerequisites, branches, recovery, and completion evidence.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The sequence explains a high-level journey or methodology without executable actions; use Process steps.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Steps remain a native ordered list, so their sequence and numbering survive without component styles.\n- Prerequisites, commands, expected results, branches, and recovery guidance remain complete in static HTML.\n- The closing statement labels the evidence that proves the whole procedure is complete.\n\nExamples: Backup and restore (`default`), Interrupted recovery (`interrupted`), Active procedure (`active`), Long procedure (`long-procedure`).\n\n### Procedure step (`procedure-step`)\n\nOne operational action with composed command and expected proof, an optional completion criterion, branch point, and explicitly labelled recovery slot.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `ProcedureStep`. Terminal: `renderProcedureStepCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- One ordered Procedure step needs an action, command, expected proof, completion criterion, branch, or recovery route.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The action stands alone and has no sequence context; use Command or ordinary prose.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Procedure supplies each step's native ordered-list position; the step does not replace that sequence with decorative numbering.\n- Commands and expected results compose the Workflow components that preserve executable input and observable proof.\n- Completion, branching, and recovery content each carries a visible text label.\n\nExamples: Command step (`default`), Branching step (`branch`), Active step (`active`).\n\n### Prerequisite list (`prerequisite-list`)\n\nRequirements named before a procedure begins, distinguishing required, satisfied, and unresolved states in visible text, shape, and semantic list order.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `PrerequisiteList`. Terminal: `renderPrerequisiteListCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Readers must verify requirements before starting an operational procedure.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The items are actions to perform in order; use Procedure steps.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Every required, satisfied, or unresolved state appears as visible text; colour and marker shape only reinforce it.\n- Requirements remain an unordered list without component styles.\n- Success colour is reserved for prerequisites that have actually been verified.\n\nExamples: Mixed prerequisites (`default`).\n\n### Branch choice (`branch-choice`)\n\nA small set of explicitly labelled routes through a procedure or next action, complete and linkable without diagram machinery or client behaviour.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `BranchChoice`. Terminal: `renderBranchChoiceCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A procedure or end-of-page next action has condition-labelled routes that readers must choose between.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The reader is moving linearly between adjacent pages (use Pager) or following ordered steps (use Procedure).\n\nAccessibility:\n- Choices and their routes remain nested lists when component styles are unavailable.\n- Linked routes use native anchors and follow document keyboard order.\n- Labels state the condition for each route instead of relying on position, arrow shape, or colour.\n\nExamples: Procedure fork (`default`), End-of-page next action (`next-action`).\n\n### Retry notice (`retry-notice`)\n\nCompact idempotence statement that says whether an interrupted action is safe to repeat and explains why.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `RetryNotice`. Terminal: `renderRetryNoticeCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A retry instruction must state whether repeating an interrupted action is safe and why.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The message is a general warning or live failure without an idempotence decision.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The immutable safe or unsafe instruction stays visible when a custom label adds context, and is never conveyed by colour alone.\n- The neutral note role avoids announcing static retry guidance as a live status.\n- Unsafe guidance uses the warning role rather than success or danger decoration.\n\nExamples: Safe to retry (`safe`), Inspect before retrying (`unsafe`).\n\n### Destructive action notice (`destructive-action-notice`)\n\nExplicit scope, impact, authority, and recovery route for an irreversible or owner-only action, with danger reserved for urgent hazards.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `DestructiveActionNotice`. Terminal: `renderDestructiveActionNoticeCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- An irreversible or owner-only action needs explicit scope, impact, authority, and recovery information.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The content is an ordinary caution, page-level announcement, or recoverable validation message.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Scope, impact, authority, and recovery are labelled definition-list facts rather than an undifferentiated warning paragraph.\n- An immutable warning or danger token stays visible when a custom label adds context; colour reinforces only that actionable hazard instead of tinting the entire block.\n- Warning uses a static note role by default; danger opts into an alert role and should be reserved for immediate hazards.\n\nExamples: Approval warning (`default`), Immediate danger (`danger`).\n\n### Result summary (`result-summary`)\n\nOne tool result fact stated in plain language, with supporting counts, duration, next action, and an optional adapter-only copy of its machine-readable form.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `ResultSummary`. Terminal: `renderResultSummaryCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- One tool result needs a plain-language fact, supporting counts, duration, or next action.\n\nDo not use when:\n- You need a durable report recording several checks and branch or commit metadata; use Verification report.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Every state appears as a visible word; semantic colour and markers only reinforce it.\n- The primary fact and optional next action remain complete text in source order without relying on layout or terminal conventions.\n- Supporting figures use a definition list, keeping each label programmatically bound to its value.\n- The adapter-only machine-data copy action announces completion politely and leaves focus on the button.\n\nExamples: Passed (`passed`), Failed (`failed`), Blocked (`blocked`), Changed (`changed`), Declared (`declared`), Unchanged (`unchanged`).\n\n### Diagnostic (`diagnostic`)\n\nStructured failure account that explains impact, exact location, evidence, reproduction and retry commands, correction, and optional raw detail.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `Diagnostic`. Terminal: `renderDiagnosticCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A specific failure needs its impact, location, evidence, reproduction, correction, and retry path kept together.\n\nDo not use when:\n- You need a short page-level announcement without reproduction or correction detail; use Banner.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Failure severity derives role=alert while attention derives role=status, unless a consumer supplies an explicit role.\n- Failure and attention appear as visible words; danger and warning colour only reinforce the actionable marker.\n- Locations compose Path reference, commands compose Command, and raw detail composes Raw output, preserving each component's accessible contract.\n- Evidence remains semantic preformatted code and scrolls horizontally instead of forcing the page wider.\n- Suggested correction is required, so a diagnostic never leaves the next action implicit.\n\nExamples: Verbose failure (`verbose-failure`), Attention (`attention`).\n\n### Raw output (`raw-output`)\n\nNative disclosure for machine-oriented detail, with a visible collapsed or expanded label and faithful horizontal overflow.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `RawOutput`. Terminal: `renderRawOutputCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Machine-oriented detail should remain available without competing with the human-readable result or diagnostic.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The output is the primary content readers need to compare or act on; show it directly with Terminal, Table, or Diagnostic.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Native details and summary semantics keep the disclosure operable without client JavaScript.\n- Collapsed and expanded are visible text labels that change with the native open state.\n- Machine output remains semantic preformatted code and scrolls in both axes instead of forcing the page wider.\n- The disclosure marker is hidden decoration, and its motion is removed when reduced motion is requested.\n\nExamples: Collapsed output (`default`), Expanded output (`expanded`).\n\n### Standard meter (`standard-meter`)\n\nQuality reading against a rising floor or falling ceiling, with current value, limit, headroom, and an optional plain-text trend.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `StandardMeter`. Terminal: `renderStandardMeterCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A quality measure must be read against an explicit rising floor or falling ceiling with visible headroom.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The value is generic progress without a defended limit; use Meter or Stat.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Within-or-outside status, current value, limit, floor-or-ceiling direction, and headroom are all visible text; the bar is supporting magnitude, not the only reading.\n- The composed Meter carries programmatic now, minimum, and maximum values.\n- Improving, drifting, and flat trends appear as visible words alongside semantic colour.\n\nExamples: Coverage floor (`default`), Density ceiling (`ceiling`).\n\n### Activity log (`activity-log`)\n\nLong-running work as one calm frame: pinned stable results above a bounded tail of the most recent streamed detail and its in-progress partial line.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: displaying-tool-output, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `ActivityLog`. Terminal: `renderActivityLogCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Minutes-long work should keep its results visible while raw detail streams beneath them.\n- A run produces both facts worth pinning and output worth glancing at, and neither should displace the other.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The output is a finished artifact to read in full; show it with Raw output or Terminal.\n- Only step statuses matter and no detail streams; Worklog is the compact statused feed.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The streamed tail is a native log role, so assistive technology can treat newly added lines as a polite live region.\n- Stable-line tones pair a distinct visible marker glyph and accessible name with their colour, so severity survives without colour perception.\n- The headline pairs its active, complete, or cancelled state with a visible glyph carrying that accessible name.\n- All text sizes stay at or above the authored interface floor, including the monospace tail.\n\nExamples: Streaming activity (`default`), Completed activity (`complete`), Cancelled activity (`cancelled`).\n\n### Artifact tree (`artifact-tree`)\n\nSemantic nested-list project tree with explicit file and directory kinds, middle-truncated names, full-path titles, and an optional annotation slot per node.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `ArtifactTree`. Terminal: `renderArtifactTreeCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A project or output structure must show nested files and directories with full paths and annotations.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The hierarchy is site navigation or the reader needs details for one artifact only.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Files and directories are nested list items, not an ASCII-art approximation.\n- Each node exposes its kind and full path as text while visual glyphs remain decorative.\n- Long names preserve both ends under truncation, and narrow layouts move annotations below the name without page overflow.\n\nExamples: Project tree (`default`), Deep generated path (`deep-tree`).\n\n### File change (`file-change`)\n\nOne file's added, updated, generated, removed, or unchanged disposition beside its full path and an optional Diffstat magnitude.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `FileChange`. Terminal: `renderFileChangeCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A changed-file list needs an explicit disposition, full path, and optional change magnitude for each file.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Readers need the file contents, raw diff, or whole-artifact provenance.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Every disposition is a visible word; the marker and semantic colour are supplementary.\n- Generated and unchanged use neutral treatment rather than warning colours.\n- The composed Path reference keeps the full path available when the visible middle truncates.\n\nExamples: Updated file (`default`), Added file (`added`), Generated file (`generated`), Removed file (`removed`), Unchanged file (`unchanged`).\n\n### Artifact card (`artifact-card`)\n\nWhole created or modified artifact with its name, Path reference, one-line summary, Ownership badge, provenance, and optional source-link slot.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `ArtifactCard`. Terminal: `renderArtifactCardCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- One created or modified artifact needs its path, summary, ownership, provenance, and source link presented together.\n\nDo not use when:\n- You need a compact row in a changed-file list or a nested project structure.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The artifact is an article with a heading and definition-list metadata.\n- Ownership remains explicit text through the composed Ownership badge.\n- The composed Path reference preserves the full path under visible truncation.\n\nExamples: Generated artifact (`default`), Project-owned artifact (`project-owned`).\n\n### Ownership badge (`ownership-badge`)\n\nInline authored, generated, project-owned, or tool-owned label that extends the Badge idiom while keeping every ownership relationship explicit in text.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `OwnershipBadge`. Terminal: `renderOwnershipBadgeCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- An artifact needs an explicit authored, generated, project-owned, or tool-owned provenance label.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The label describes status, category, or arbitrary metadata rather than ownership; use Badge.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Every ownership relationship is visible text; colour, border style, and typeface are supplementary.\n- Generated and tool-owned are neutral provenance facts rather than warning states.\n\nExamples: Authored (`authored`), Generated (`generated`), Project owned (`project-owned`), Tool owned (`tool-owned`).\n\n### Decision record (`decision-record`)\n\nArchitecture decision record rendered as an honest article with title, accepted or superseded status, date, context, decision, and consequences.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `DecisionRecord`. Terminal: `renderDecisionRecordCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A significant decision needs its status, date, context, chosen direction, and consequences recorded together.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The content is a binding instruction without decision history; use Rule.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Status is visible text in the composed Badge and never relies on colour.\n- The record date uses a semantic time element with a machine-readable value.\n- Context, decision, and consequences are labelled sections in source order.\n\nExamples: Accepted decision (`default`), Superseded decision (`superseded`).\n\n### Rule (`rule`)\n\nOne binding project instruction with explicit origin and scope, presented as neutral authority rather than warning or diagnostic chrome.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Rule`. Terminal: `renderRuleCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A binding instruction must state its origin and scope as neutral project authority.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The content explains why a direction was chosen; use Decision record.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The binding instruction, origin, and scope are all visible text in source order.\n- The accent edge reinforces authority without carrying meaning on its own.\n\nExamples: Generated-file rule (`default`), Namespaced-style rule (`namespaced-styles`).\n\n### Task metadata (`task-metadata`)\n\nQuiet task-page orientation strip that labels outcome, audience, prerequisites, complexity, file effects, retry safety, and expected end state.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `TaskMetadata`. Terminal: `renderTaskMetadataCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- An operational page must orient the reader before its procedure with a complete set of task facts.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The task has already run and the page needs to summarize its outcome, counts, or duration; use Result summary.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Every fact is paired with its visible term through definition-list semantics.\n- File effects and retry safety are complete text, never colour-only states.\n- The strip remains in source order and keeps every value visible when component styles are unavailable.\n\nExamples: Read-only task (`default`), File-changing task (`file-changing`).\n\n### Agent handoff (`agent-handoff`)\n\nSelf-contained prose instructions for an agent session, with one visible string serving as the exact adapter-only clipboard payload.\n\nGroup: Workflow. Purposes: building-documentation, procedural-workflow.\nReact: `AgentHandoff`. Terminal: `renderAgentHandoffCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A task page must provide complete plain-text instructions that a reader can inspect and paste into an agent session unchanged.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The copied input is a shell command to run or machine-readable output from a completed tool invocation.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The complete prompt remains visible as wrapping preformatted prose without JavaScript or component styles.\n- The prompt string is the single authority for both visible content and clipboard text, so hidden chrome cannot contaminate the copy.\n- The copy action announces completion politely and retains keyboard focus on its button.\n\nExamples: Review handoff (`default`), Long wrapping prompt (`long-prompt`).\n\n## Docs\n\n### Skip link (`skip-link`)\n\nVisually hidden bypass link that surfaces on keyboard focus and jumps to the main content.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: none.\nReact: `SkipLink`. Terminal: exempt — Browser focus-bypass navigation has no terminal document equivalent because terminal output is already linear.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Hidden with a clip-path technique that keeps the link in the accessibility tree and the tab order.\n- On focus it surfaces as the page's topmost element with a visible outline, meeting the bypass-blocks expectation.\n\nExamples: Keyboard bypass (`default`, web only).\n\n### Docs header (`docs-header`)\n\nSticky opaque documentation top bar with brand, middle, and action regions.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `DocsHeader`. Terminal: `renderDocsHeaderCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The header element is a banner landmark; slotted controls keep their own semantics and focus outlines.\n- The translucent surface keeps token-driven text contrast over scrolled content in both themes.\n\nExamples: Documentation header (`default`).\n\n### Docs nav (`docs-nav`)\n\nSectioned documentation navigation with explicit current page or location semantics.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `DocsNav`. Terminal: `renderDocsNavCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- A configurable label names the navigation landmark, and each section stays a real list.\n- Adjacent destinations form one contiguous pointer run, so moving through a list never leaves the links' hit targets.\n- Each current destination carries the appropriate page or location value and reinforces its highlight with weight, not colour alone.\n\nExamples: Section navigation (`default`).\n\n### Pager (`pager`)\n\nSequential previous/next navigation between adjacent pages in a reading order.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Pager`. Terminal: `renderPagerCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- A configurable label names the navigation landmark, distinct from the page's primary navigation.\n- Direction words are real text inside each link, so the announced name carries both direction and destination.\n\nExamples: Previous and next (`default`), Next page only (`next-only`).\n\n### Search palette (`search-palette`)\n\nModal command-palette search on the native dialog element, with a search field, results region, and hint row.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `SearchPalette`. Terminal: exempt — Modal focus management, query input, and result activation belong to an interactive terminal driver.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The native dialog provides focus containment, Escape dismissal, and focus return to the opening control.\n- The search input is name-labelled and focused on open; results are real links, not synthetic listbox options.\n- A visible Close action provides an explicit pointer and keyboard dismissal path in addition to Escape and backdrop dismissal.\n- Escape follows the platform search-input convention: it clears a non-empty query first, and closes the palette when the query is empty.\n\nExamples: Search dialog (`default`, web only).\n\n### Anchor heading (`anchor-heading`)\n\nHeading with a hover-revealed self link for direct section linking.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `AnchorHeading`. Terminal: `renderAnchorHeadingCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The self link carries a configurable descriptive label rather than the bare section-mark glyph.\n- The link stays keyboard reachable while visually hidden, becomes visible on focus, and is always visible on hoverless devices.\n\nExamples: Section heading (`default`), Nested heading (`nested-heading`).\n\n### Kbd (`kbd`)\n\nKeycap-styled display of one keyboard key or chord segment using the semantic kbd element.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Kbd`. Terminal: `renderKbdCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The semantic kbd element identifies keyboard input to assistive technology without extra ARIA.\n- Keycap text uses the interface face at the interface-text floor size and holds contrast in both themes.\n\nExamples: Single key (`default`), Key chord (`key-chord`).\n\n### Copy button (`copy-button`)\n\nClipboard copy button with a transient copied state, an optional icon slot, and a polite announcement.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `CopyButton`. Terminal: exempt — Clipboard mutation and transient confirmation require an interactive driver, not a pure terminal renderer.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The label swap between copy and copied is announced through a polite live region.\n- The copied state is carried by a data attribute, so colour is reinforced by the announced text rather than colour alone.\n\nExamples: Copy action (`default`, web only).\n\n### Glossary term (`glossary-term`)\n\nInline definition term with a keyboard-reachable, dotted-underlined hover card.\n\nGroup: Docs. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `GlossaryTerm`. Terminal: `renderGlossaryTermCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The semantic dfn trigger is always focusable, so the same definition available on hover is available from the keyboard and on touch focus.\n- The trigger and card are connected through aria-details; the visible term is repeated as the card heading for context.\n- Definition accepts phrasing content for valid placement inside prose; use Hover card block layout when the supplementary content needs headings, lists, or actions.\n\nExamples: Inline definition (`default`).\n\n## Marketing\n\n### Marketing section (`marketing-section`)\n\nOpt-in campaign-page section with durable frame, rhythm, and contrast scopes.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `MarketingSection`. Terminal: exempt — Its contract is a browser page-width, spacing, and background scope; terminal Marketing renderers own their width and hierarchy within each frame instead.\n\nUse when:\n- A campaign or landing page needs a repeatable wide frame, generous section rhythm, or a stable dark contrast chapter.\n\nDo not use when:\n- An application or ordinary editorial page only needs a semantic surface and standard spacing; use Section with Container.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The contrast surface remaps semantic ink, surface, and border roles together so descendant headings and copy remain readable in both themes.\n- The component preserves a native section landmark and leaves heading hierarchy to its content.\n\nExamples: Standard canvas (`standard`, web only), Wide spacious contrast (`spacious-contrast`, web only).\n\n### Site header (`site-header`)\n\nResponsive landing-page masthead with standard and campaign treatments, optional notice, navigation, actions, and sticky positioning.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `SiteHeader`. Terminal: exempt — Sticky responsive website mastheads and page navigation are browser chrome; terminal applications expose their own command and interaction navigation rather than a site header.\n\nUse when:\n- A public page needs persistent brand, section navigation, and a primary action; use the campaign variant for a wider translucent launch-page masthead.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Navigation has a configurable accessible label.\n- At narrow widths links remain available in a horizontally scrollable row in both variants.\n\nExamples: Standard (`standard`, web only), Campaign (`campaign`, web only).\n\n### Marketing intro (`marketing-intro`)\n\nEyebrow, headline, and standfirst composition for marketing-page sections.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `MarketingIntro`. Terminal: exempt — Terminal Marketing renderers already share the equivalent text hierarchy through their frame helper; this component's distinct contract is browser measure, alignment, and responsive display scale.\n\nUse when:\n- A marketing section needs a repeatable eyebrow, headline, and standfirst hierarchy at ordinary or campaign scale.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The composition opens a page with actions or a visual; use Hero block.\n- The composition opens long-form editorial content with bylines and metadata; use Article header.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The heading rank is explicit so the intro can preserve the surrounding document hierarchy.\n- Contrast tone keeps the eyebrow, title, and supporting copy legible on a stable dark surface in either theme.\n\nExamples: Editorial scale (`editorial`, web only), Centred contrast (`contrast`, web only).\n\n### Hero block (`hero-block`)\n\nHigh-impact opening section with split, centered, and showcase compositions plus flexible action, proof, visual, and decorative-backdrop slots.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `HeroBlock`. Terminal: `renderHeroBlockCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A campaign needs one opening promise with actions and optional visual evidence; use showcase with the atmospheric surface for publication-scale launches.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The heading level is explicit so the block can open a page or a nested campaign.\n- Copy, actions, and supporting visuals retain source order when the layout collapses.\n- The backdrop slot is always hidden from assistive technology and remains behind complete foreground content.\n- Showcase emphasis uses real title text and remains readable when gradient clipping is unavailable.\n\nExamples: Split accent (`split`), Showcase (`showcase`), Artwork backdrop (`backdrop`, web only).\n\n### Logo cloud (`logo-cloud`)\n\nQuiet grid or loose campaign strip for customer, partner, integration, or publication marks.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `LogoCloud`. Terminal: `renderLogoCloudCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A page needs readable organization names beside optional decorative marks; use strip for a lighter provider or integration line.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Marks are decorative by default while every item retains a readable text name.\n- An optional mark mask preserves supplied image artwork in light Theme and replaces it completely with a neutral silhouette in dark Theme, without replacing the readable name.\n\nExamples: Trust grid (`grid`), Provider strip (`strip`).\n\n### Audience grid (`audience-grid`)\n\nPersona-led card grid for explaining one product through the outcomes different audiences care about.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `AudienceGrid`. Terminal: `renderAudienceGridCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Audience cards use a section heading followed by independently headed articles.\n- Decorative numerals and icons are hidden from assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Audience paths (`default`).\n\n### Feature bento (`feature-bento`)\n\nStrict rectangular feature matrix with fixed footprints, surfaces, icons, and visual slots.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `FeatureBento`. Terminal: exempt — Its distinguishing contract is a strict responsive size-and-media matrix; flattening it to terminal text would discard the component's defining structure.\n\nUse when:\n- A curated feature set has an intentional hierarchy and can fill a complete rectangular matrix.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Items have natural unrelated heights or cannot form a complete matrix; use Masonry or Grid instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Explicit placement preserves article source order and never backfills later items into earlier cells.\n- The matrix becomes a single source-order column on narrow viewports.\n- Icons are decorative; each feature carries a visible heading and description.\n\nExamples: Lead matrix (`lead-matrix`, web only), Vertical matrix (`vertical-matrix`, web only).\n\n### Split feature (`split-feature`)\n\nAlternating editorial feature section with narrative copy, proof points, actions, and an unconstrained media slot.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: none.\nReact: `SplitFeature`. Terminal: exempt — Its identity depends on alternating copy beside an unconstrained media slot; a terminal cannot render that arbitrary media relationship without collapsing into other text-first marketing components.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Content precedes media in source order even when the visual position is reversed.\n- Proof points are expressed as a semantic list.\n\nExamples: Evidence split (`default`, web only).\n\n### Process steps (`process-steps`)\n\nNumbered horizontal or vertical journey for onboarding, workflow, implementation, or methodology stories.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `ProcessSteps`. Terminal: `renderProcessStepsCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A marketing or onboarding page needs to explain a high-level journey or methodology.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Readers must perform executable, ordered actions with prerequisites, branches, recovery, and proof; use Procedure.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Steps are an ordered list, so sequence remains explicit without the visual connectors.\n\nExamples: In progress (`default`), Validation error (`error`).\n\n### Metrics band (`metrics-band`)\n\nCompact evidence strip for a handful of high-signal outcomes, with surface, accent, and contrast treatments.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: none.\nReact: `MetricsBand`. Terminal: `renderMetricsBandCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Metrics use a description list so each figure stays paired with its label.\n\nExamples: Measured outcomes (`default`).\n\n### Comparison table (`comparison-table`)\n\nThree-column capability comparison with an emphasized recommendation and card-like mobile rows.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `ComparisonTable`. Terminal: `renderComparisonTableCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Column and row headers preserve table relationships for assistive technology.\n- Mobile labels are derived from the same column names rather than duplicated visually.\n\nExamples: Comparison (`default`).\n\n### Testimonial (`testimonial`)\n\nEditorial customer quote with attribution, optional portrait, and an adjacent measurable outcome.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Testimonial`. Terminal: `renderTestimonialCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Quotation and attribution use blockquote and figcaption semantics.\n- Decorative quote marks and avatars are hidden from assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Wide quote (`default`).\n\n### Case study (`case-study`)\n\nLong-form proof block pairing a customer narrative with visual evidence and compact outcome metrics.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: none.\nReact: `CaseStudy`. Terminal: `renderCaseStudyCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The story is an article and the supporting figures use a description list.\n\nExamples: Proof story (`default`).\n\n### FAQ block (`faq-block`)\n\nEditorial frequently-asked-questions section using native disclosure controls and a sticky introduction.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: none.\nReact: `FaqBlock`. Terminal: `renderFaqBlockCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Questions use native details and summary elements, so disclosure works without JavaScript.\n- Keyboard focus receives a visible accent outline.\n\nExamples: First answer open (`default`).\n\n### CTA band (`cta-band`)\n\nHigh-emphasis closing invitation with centered or split layouts, three surface treatments, and a visual slot.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `CtaBand`. Terminal: `renderCtaBandCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The call to action remains a headed section and accepts ordinary link or button controls.\n\nExamples: Accent call to action (`default`).\n\n### Site footer (`site-footer`)\n\nResponsive page colophon with product context, grouped navigation, legal copy, and a compact metadata rail.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `SiteFooter`. Terminal: exempt — This is browser page chrome for grouped site links, legal copy, and a responsive colophon; a CLI owns shell help and exit context instead of rendering a website footer.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Grouped links sit inside a labelled footer navigation landmark.\n- Navigation group titles preserve a useful heading outline.\n\nExamples: Link groups (`default`, web only).\n\n### Marketing stage (`marketing-stage`)\n\nQuiet, consistent framing for conceptual, atmospheric, or evidential marketing visuals.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `MarketingStage`. Terminal: exempt — Its contract is browser media proportion, material framing, and visual overflow; terminal renderers frame their own deterministic text directly.\n\nUse when:\n- A marketing composition needs one restrained frame for artwork, a diagram, media, or a concise piece of evidence.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The supplied content is primarily prose or a long terminal transcript; keep it in the reading flow instead of presenting it as imagery.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The optional caption uses native figure semantics and remains associated with the supplied visual content.\n- The component does not infer whether supplied artwork is meaningful or decorative; consumers retain responsibility for the child content's accessible alternative.\n\nExamples: Framed concept (`framed`, web only), Inset atmosphere (`inset`, web only), Plain artwork (`plain`, web only).\n\n### Editorial hero (`editorial-hero`)\n\nSelective campaign-page opening with a broad editorial statement and an optional low-burden visual stage.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `EditorialHero`. Terminal: exempt — Its distinguishing contract is browser-scale editorial proportion, atmospheric layering, and a wide visual stage; a terminal opening should use a concise native heading frame instead.\n\nUse when:\n- A page needs one decisive opening statement whose supporting visual compresses or relieves the copy rather than adding another body of text.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The composition sits midway through a page or needs ordinary heading scale; use Narrative chapter or Marketing intro.\n- The only available visual is a dense transcript or code listing; omit the visual or reduce it to a concise piece of evidence first.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The heading rank is explicit so the component can open a page or a nested campaign.\n- The visual follows the explanatory copy in source order when the composition collapses.\n- Decorative backdrop content is always hidden from assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Conceptual visual (`visual`, web only), Text only (`text-only`, web only).\n\n### Narrative chapter (`narrative-chapter`)\n\nCalm two-part reading section for explaining a substantial idea without adding visual burden.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `NarrativeChapter`. Terminal: exempt — Its distinguishing contract is a responsive browser reading measure with a parallel editorial introduction and optional aside; terminal prose should remain in one linear reading flow.\n\nUse when:\n- A landing page must explain a complex idea in several paragraphs and needs hierarchy, measure, and relief more than another product visual.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The section can be understood as a short sequence, comparison, or single outcome; use a component that compresses the information instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The introduction precedes the complete reading flow in source order.\n- The optional supporting context uses a labelled native aside and remains supplementary when the layout becomes one column.\n- The heading rank is explicit so the chapter preserves the surrounding document outline.\n\nExamples: Extended explanation (`extended`, web only), Concise explanation (`concise`, web only).\n\n### Journey overview (`journey-overview`)\n\nSpacious ordered overview that compresses a journey into a few plain-language moments.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `JourneyOverview`. Terminal: exempt — Its distinguishing contract is a page-scale responsive rhythm that turns a short ordered list into visual compression; terminal flows already communicate sequence directly through ordered text.\n\nUse when:\n- A complex process can be honestly summarized into a few audience-facing moments with one clear outcome each.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Readers must execute detailed instructions, inspect technical state, or recover from branches; use Procedure or a dedicated workflow component instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Steps use a native ordered list, so their sequence does not depend on decorative indices or the desktop grid.\n- Each step keeps its title, explanation, and optional outcome together when the layout collapses.\n\nExamples: Three-step journey (`three-step`, web only), Compact journey (`compact`, web only).\n\n### Outcome spotlight (`outcome-spotlight`)\n\nEvidence chapter that asks the reader to remember one outcome and only a few supporting facts.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `OutcomeSpotlight`. Terminal: exempt — Its distinguishing contract is the browser-scale contrast between one dominant result and a restrained supporting rail; terminal evidence should render as compact semantic facts without display hierarchy.\n\nUse when:\n- One result deserves to carry the section and no more than a few short facts genuinely strengthen it.\n\nDo not use when:\n- All figures have equal importance or require detailed qualification; use Metrics band, Table, or prose instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Primary and supporting facts use description lists so every value remains paired with its explanation.\n- The title precedes the evidence in source order and its rank is explicit.\n- Contrast is inherited from Marketing section's complete semantic role remapping.\n\nExamples: Focused outcome (`focused`, web only), Textual outcome (`textual`, web only).\n\n### Voice break (`voice-break`)\n\nLow-burden change of voice that lets one concise quotation interrupt a demanding page.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `VoiceBreak`. Terminal: exempt — Its contract is browser page rhythm and the spatial contrast between a large quotation and compact attribution; terminals can quote prose without a dedicated renderer.\n\nUse when:\n- A text-heavy page needs a brief human perspective or memorable sentence before returning to explanation.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The quotation needs a metric, long customer story, or substantial evidence; use Testimonial or Case study instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Quotation and attribution use native blockquote and figcaption semantics.\n- The optional portrait is decorative because the readable attribution remains complete without it.\n- The quotation precedes its attribution in the narrow visual layout while remaining part of the same figure.\n\nExamples: Calm voice (`calm`, web only), Contrast voice (`contrast`, web only).\n\n### Closing statement (`closing-statement`)\n\nCalm final chapter with one proposition, a focused action area, and optional reassurance.\n\nGroup: Marketing. Purposes: marketing-site.\nReact: `ClosingStatement`. Terminal: exempt — Its distinguishing contract is a browser page-ending chapter with broad editorial scale, centered action layout, and atmospheric backdrop; terminal flows close with their own prompt or next command.\n\nUse when:\n- A long page should resolve into one proposition and one obvious action without introducing another feature list.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The invitation needs multiple offers, pricing choices, or a substantial visual; use a dedicated decision or comparison component instead.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The heading rank is explicit and precedes the action area in source order.\n- Decorative backdrop content is always hidden from assistive technology.\n- Actions remain ordinary consumer-supplied links or buttons and stack without changing order at narrow widths.\n\nExamples: Contrast close (`contrast`, web only), Quiet close (`quiet`, web only).\n\n## Editorial\n\n### Article header (`article-header`)\n\nPublication-scale opening for essays, reports, guides, and premium long-form pages, with byline, metadata, actions, and optional cover media.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `ArticleHeader`. Terminal: `renderArticleHeaderCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The heading level is explicit so the opener can lead a page or sit inside a larger publication.\n- Author information uses address semantics and decorative initials stay hidden from assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Article opener (`default`).\n\n### Article layout (`article-layout`)\n\nResponsive long-form reading shell with optional navigation and contextual rail around a primary article column.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `ArticleLayout`. Terminal: `renderArticleLayoutCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The primary reading stream is an article; optional rails are labelled complementary landmarks.\n- Source order keeps navigation, article, and supporting context understandable without the visual grid.\n\nExamples: Contextual reading layout (`default`).\n\n### Table of contents (`table-of-contents`)\n\nCompact article navigation with numbered sections, unnumbered nested entries, an optional reading-progress note, and a clear current-location state.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `TableOfContents`. Terminal: `renderTableOfContentsCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The component is a labelled navigation landmark and exposes the current location with aria-current.\n- Nested entries remain visible text and omit a misleading section number.\n\nExamples: Article contents (`default`).\n\n### Prose (`prose`)\n\nLong-form reading context that composes semantic paragraphs and structural blocks at a shared measure without parsing or flattening them.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `Prose`. Terminal: `renderProseCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Several paragraphs, headings, lists, quotations, or code blocks need one readable long-form measure and optional opening treatment.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Paragraph when one semantic unit of body prose is the complete content.\n- Use Heading when the content introduces document hierarchy rather than supplying a reading context.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The adapter adds no document hierarchy; authors keep full control of semantic headings and content order.\n- Readable measure and generous line height persist across all variants.\n- The terminal rich path preserves child Component structure, inline meaning, hyperlink targets, hard breaks, and paragraph boundaries without relying on colour.\n\nExamples: Lead prose (`default`), Rich document structure (`rich-structure`).\n\n### Markdown (`markdown`)\n\nSafe CommonMark/GFM document rendering through one neutral parser model and the package's semantic browser and terminal Components.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Markdown`. Terminal: `renderMarkdownCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A consumer owns Markdown source and needs complete, safe browser or terminal document output without owning parser presentation logic.\n- A standard Markdown image names a generated diagram or chart asset and an explicit source-to-spec resource should upgrade it on package-owned surfaces.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Prose when the caller already owns semantic React or CLI block structure rather than Markdown source.\n- Use Paragraph when one already-semantic unit of phrasing is the complete content.\n- Use Code block for literal source that must not be parsed as Markdown.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Native headings, lists, quotations, tables, code, images, and end-note relationships preserve the document hierarchy in source order.\n- Heading ids are stable and duplicate-safe; footnote references and returns are descriptive keyboard-reachable links.\n- Raw HTML is inert, unsafe destinations remain visible but non-clickable, and source controls become visible notation.\n- Admitted isolated diagram and chart images must use the spec-derived alternative and matching optional summary title; drift rejects the whole document.\n\nExamples: Compact document (`default`), Full dialect (`full-dialect`), Deep nesting (`deep-nesting`), Reading hierarchy (`reading-hierarchy`), Diagram resource (`diagram-resource`), Chart resource (`chart-resource`), Inert hostile source (`hostile-source`), Narrow layout (`narrow-layout`).\n\n### Paragraph (`paragraph`)\n\nOne semantic paragraph with package reading typography and safe, width-aware rich inline content in terminals.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `Paragraph`. Terminal: `renderParagraphCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- One prose paragraph is the semantic unit, including rich phrasing such as emphasis, links, and inline code.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Prose when several paragraphs, headings, and lists need one long-form reading context.\n- Use Callout when the content is a highlighted aside rather than ordinary body prose.\n- Use Heading when the text introduces a section in the document hierarchy.\n- Use Code listing for source with line structure, or an ordinary preformatted element when whitespace must remain arbitrary.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The React adapter renders a native paragraph and leaves phrasing semantics to its children.\n- The terminal renderer retains code delimiters, link targets, image alternatives and sources, and footnote markers without relying on colour.\n\nExamples: Rich inline paragraph (`default`).\n\n### List (`list`)\n\nNeutral unordered, ordered, and read-only task lists with rich inline content and nested structural blocks.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `List`. Terminal: `renderListCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Content is an ordinary unordered, ordered, mixed, nested, or read-only task list without a narrower editorial or workflow meaning.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Procedure for executable operational steps with completion evidence.\n- Use Prerequisite list for requirements whose readiness state must be explicit.\n- Use Key points for a curated editorial summary rather than an ordinary list.\n- Use Checkbox for an interactive form control; List task markers are disabled, read-only document state.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The React adapter preserves native unordered or ordered list and list-item semantics at every nesting level.\n- Task markers are disabled, read-only native checkboxes; items without a task state remain ordinary list items.\n- Terminal task states remain distinct through marker shape in Unicode, ASCII, and no-colour output.\n\nExamples: Nested list (`default`), Mixed task list (`task-mixed`).\n\n### Key points (`key-points`)\n\nScannable article summary that turns a small set of central ideas into a numbered editorial brief.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `KeyPoints`. Terminal: `renderKeyPointsCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Key ideas are an ordered list with real headings rather than a visually numbered collection of generic containers.\n\nExamples: Editorial brief (`default`).\n\n### Blockquote (`blockquote`)\n\nA neutral block quotation that preserves composed document structure without adding attribution or editorial framing.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `Blockquote`. Terminal: `renderBlockquoteCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Quoted material contains one or more ordinary semantic blocks, including nested lists, headings, code, or quotations.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Pull quote when the quotation needs authored quote marks, attribution, citation, or a display treatment.\n- Use Callout when the content is a highlighted note with a semantic tone rather than quoted material.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The React adapter renders a native blockquote and preserves the semantics of every child block.\n- The terminal renderer repeats its capability-aware rail on every line, including intentional blank lines, without relying on colour for quotation meaning.\n\nExamples: Composed quotation (`default`), Nested quotation (`nested-quotation`).\n\n### Pull quote (`pull-quote`)\n\nTypographic quotation treatment that can stay within the reading measure or break wide for a deliberate change of pace.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `PullQuote`. Terminal: `renderPullQuoteCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Quotation and attribution retain blockquote, figure, figcaption, and cite semantics.\n- The oversized quotation mark is decorative and hidden from assistive technology.\n\nExamples: Attributed quotation (`default`).\n\n### Callout (`callout`)\n\nInset editorial note for context, interpretation, cautions, and successful outcomes without breaking the reading flow.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Callout`. Terminal: `renderCalloutCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The callout is exposed as a note landmark with a real heading and never relies on colour alone for meaning.\n\nExamples: Insight (`default`), Warning (`warning`).\n\n### Code block (`code-block`)\n\nLiteral, non-truncating preformatted code with optional language information, a specimen frame, and a lossless terminal wrapping policy.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `CodeBlock`. Terminal: `renderCodeBlockCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Source whitespace and every code character must remain available without line numbers, a caption, or an executable terminal session.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use Code listing when source needs an editorial figure, filename, caption, highlighted lines, or stable line references.\n- Use Terminal for a command transcript or recognisable shell session rather than source code.\n- Use Raw output for collapsible machine output attached to an operational workflow.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The React adapter renders native preformatted code, preserves the literal source as text content, and fixes non-ASCII fallback glyphs to their measured terminal cells.\n- Language and parser information are exposed through discern-namespaced data hooks without assuming a syntax highlighter.\n- The terminal renderer expands tabs to four-cell tab stops, makes unsafe control and format characters visible, and marks lossless continuations in its specimen frame without relying on colour.\n\nExamples: TypeScript source (`default`), Preserved long line (`preserved-width`).\n\n### Code listing (`code-listing`)\n\nCaptioned source listing with standard and campaign showcase treatments, file and language context, stable line numbers, horizontal overflow, and optional highlighted lines.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation, marketing-site.\nReact: `CodeListing`. Terminal: `renderCodeListingCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- A reader needs source with stable line references; use showcase when the listing is visual evidence inside a campaign page.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Source remains semantic preformatted code; line numbers and highlights do not alter its readable text.\n- Long lines scroll horizontally rather than being visually reflowed into invalid code.\n- Showcase keeps the same semantic code and caption while using stable inverse roles in both themes.\n\nExamples: Standard (`standard`), Showcase (`showcase`).\n\n### Data figure (`data-figure`)\n\nFramed figure for charts, diagrams, annotated images, and research evidence, with legend, caption, and source slots.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `DataFigure`. Terminal: `renderDataFigureCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The caller supplies the visual's accessible representation while the surrounding title, caption, legend, and source retain figure semantics.\n- Legend labels accompany every colour swatch so colour is never the only key.\n\nExamples: Comparison figure (`default`), Narrow layout (`narrow-layout`).\n\n### Chart (`chart`)\n\nAccessible token-themed SVG projection for package-owned quantitative chart kinds.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Chart`. Terminal: `renderChartCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Measured quantities on scales are meaningful reference information that must survive browser, standalone SVG, and terminal projections.\n- A typed package-owned chart kind describes the data without caller coordinates, renderer options, or drawing instructions.\n\nDo not use when:\n- A surrounding title, caption, source, or legend is required; compose Chart as DataFigure's visual with its spec-derived series legend instead of duplicating figure semantics.\n- The subject is identity and topology rather than quantities on scales; a semantic Diagram kind serves it.\n- One headline number or a single progress fact is the whole story; Stat and Meter own those treatments.\n- The consumer owns a bespoke visualization whose meaning cannot be expressed by a built-in chart kind.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The spec's required title and summary name the SVG while its kind-specific structural description preserves every authored series, category, value, declared gap, and mechanical extreme.\n- Title and summary are accessibility context, not visible canvas headings; surrounding document components own visible editorial prose.\n- Every series slot pairs its colour with a stable non-colour identity — authored order, per-slot marker and fill glyphs, and a machine-tested colour-vision separation floor — so colour never carries a series alone.\n\nExamples: Representative chart (`default`), Structural variation (`structural`), Dense data (`dense-data`).\n\n### Diagram (`diagram`)\n\nAccessible token-themed SVG projection for package-owned semantic diagram kinds.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation, displaying-tool-output.\nReact: `Diagram`. Terminal: `renderDiagramCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- The relationships between authored entities are meaningful reference information that must survive browser, standalone SVG, and terminal projections.\n- A typed package-owned diagram kind describes the subject without caller coordinates or drawing instructions.\n\nDo not use when:\n- A surrounding title, caption, source, or legend is required; compose Diagram as DataFigure's visual instead of duplicating figure semantics.\n- The visual is semantically disposable decoration; use Artwork with an empty alternative instead.\n- The consumer owns a bespoke illustration or interactive explainer whose meaning cannot be expressed by a built-in diagram kind.\n- Intuition, arbitrary coordinates, rich labels, or custom drawing callbacks would replace a cited or authored reference model.\n\nAccessibility:\n- The spec's required title and summary name the SVG while its kind-specific structural description preserves every authored fact, annotation, relationship, order, boundary, date, and direction.\n- Title and summary are accessibility context, not visible canvas headings; surrounding document components own visible editorial prose.\n- Semantic roles pair colour with labels, shapes, border treatment, or line treatment so colour is never the only distinction.\n\nExamples: Representative topology (`default`), Dense topology (`dense-topology`).\n\n### Timeline (`timeline`)\n\nChronological narrative for histories, release stories, investigations, and staged programmes, with optional status and detail.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `Timeline`. Terminal: `renderTimelineCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Events remain an ordered list and visual marker status supplements rather than replaces the written content.\n\nExamples: Editorial timeline (`default`).\n\n### Footnotes (`footnotes`)\n\nEnd-note definitions with stable identities, rich multi-block bodies, and one or more explicit return links.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: building-documentation.\nReact: `Footnotes`. Terminal: `renderFootnotesCli`.\n\nUse when:\n- Definitions complete inline footnote references and may contain rich phrasing, paragraphs, lists, quotations, or code.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Use List for an ordinary sequence whose items do not define bidirectional document references.\n- Use Prose or Paragraph for reading content that is not an end-note definition.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Notes remain a native ordered list with unique stable ids, and every return link carries a descriptive accessible label.\n- Repeated references receive separate return targets in source order rather than collapsing to one ambiguous link.\n- Terminal definitions retain visible positional labels and return targets in no-colour and ASCII output.\n\nExamples: Source notes (`default`), Rich multi-block notes (`rich-multi-block`).\n\n### Related content (`related-content`)\n\nContinuation band for related essays, guides, reports, or issues, with enough context to make each next-reading choice meaningful.\n\nGroup: Editorial. Purposes: none.\nReact: `RelatedContent`. Terminal: `renderRelatedContentCli`.\n\nUse when: Metadata states no situation narrower than the description.\n\nDo not use when: Metadata records no refusal; weigh the sibling Components in this Group.\n\nAccessibility:\n- Every recommendation is a headed article and its title is the primary descriptive link.\n\nExamples: Related reading (`default`).\n";
